// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0
//
// timer.curlee — a polled clock on PIT channel 0. No interrupts.
//
//   timer_init(pm)             program the PIT, start the clock at 0
//   timer_now(pm)              microseconds since timer_init
//   timer_wait_until(pm, t)    spin until timer_now() >= t, return it
//
// Hardware. Channel 0 of the 8253/8254 counts down at 1193182 Hz, one
// tick every 838 ns. timer_init sets it to mode 2 (rate generator) with
// reload 0, which the chip reads as 65536, so the counter runs
// 65536 .. 1 and starts over every 54.9 ms. A read is a latch command on
// port 0x43 and two byte reads of port 0x40.
//
// Resolution. The counter changes every 838 ns; timer_now() reports
// whole microseconds. The unit is microseconds because that is what a
// game loop reasons in (a 60 Hz frame is 16666.7 us) and it does not
// tie callers to this chip's tick. The tick-to-us conversion is one
// multiply and a split at bit 32 (kernel/timer_math.curlee), so a poll
// costs the three port accesses plus a few dozen instructions and no
// division. On the 32-bit GRUB build the 64-bit multiply and the shifts
// compile to inline instructions; no libgcc helper is called.
//
// Monotonic. The clock only moves through timer_us_after(), whose
// contract is `result >= us`, so two calls never return a smaller value
// the second time. Its second bound, at most 54925 us per poll, follows
// from the 16-bit counter.
//
// Polling limit. A 16-bit counter cannot say how many times it wrapped
// between two reads. timer_now() takes the distance from the previous
// read modulo 65536, so the clock is exact only if the gap between two
// calls is under one period: 65536 ticks, 54.9 ms. A longer gap is not
// detected. The clock loses whole periods (a 60 ms gap counts as 5 ms),
// stays monotonic, and runs late by the lost time from then on; deadlines
// are measured on that clock, so a frame schedule slips by the same
// amount and keeps its rate. An application that polls once per 60 Hz
// frame (16.7 ms) is inside the limit while no frame takes longer than
// 54.9 ms. The same loss happens if the host stops the virtual CPU for
// that long while the PIT keeps running. timer_wait_until polls back to
// back (a few microseconds apart), far inside the limit.
//
// Verification. Module-level statics are opaque to the verifier, so the
// monotonic contract sits on the pure update in timer_math.curlee, and
// timer_now stores and returns its result. timer_wait_until proves that
// it returns only once the target is reached. This file is verified
// through the merged kernel, like vbe.curlee.

static timer_last: Int = 0;
static timer_frac: Int = 0;
static timer_us: Int = 0;

// Latch channel 0 and read its 16-bit count (a down-counter).
fn timer_read_count(pm: cap phys.mem) -> Int {
  unsafe {
    port_outb(0x43, 0);
    let lo: U8 = port_inb(0x40);
    let hi: U8 = port_inb(0x40);
    return lo + hi * 256;
  }
}

// Channel 0, lobyte/hibyte access, mode 2, binary counting (0x34), then
// the reload value 0 (65536). The clock starts at 0 from the first read.
fn timer_init(pm: cap phys.mem) -> Unit {
  unsafe {
    port_outb(0x43, 0x34);
    port_outb(0x40, 0);
    port_outb(0x40, 0);
  }
  timer_last = timer_read_count(pm);
  timer_frac = 0;
  timer_us = 0;
  return;
}

// Microseconds since timer_init. Call at least once per 54.9 ms.
fn timer_now(pm: cap phys.mem) -> Int {
  let count: Int = timer_read_count(pm);
  let us: Int = timer_us_after(timer_us, timer_frac, timer_last, count);
  let frac: Int = timer_frac_after(timer_frac, timer_last, count);
  timer_us = us;
  timer_frac = frac;
  timer_last = count;
  return us;
}

// Spin until timer_now() reaches `target` and return that reading. It
// returns at once when the target has passed, and it never returns early.
fn timer_wait_until(pm: cap phys.mem, target: Int) -> Int
  [ ensures result >= target; ] {
  while (true) {
    let now: Int = timer_now(pm);
    if (now >= target) {
      return now;
    }
  }
}
